/-!
# Quot functor of a separated morphism of finite presentation is an algebraic space (Stacks 09TU)

Let `f : X ⟶ B` be a morphism of algebraic spaces over a scheme `S` of a big fppf site which is of
finite presentation, and let `F` be a quasi-coherent `𝒪_X`-module: the standing data
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.QuotSituation` of Stacks 09TR. Stacks 09TU asserts:

1. if `f` is separated, then `Quot_{F/X/B}` is an algebraic space;
2. if moreover `F` is of finite presentation, then `Quot_{F/X/B} ⟶ B` is locally of finite
   presentation.

A section of the Quot presheaf over a scheme of the site is recorded by a subobject, so it lives
one universe above the site; the statement is about the models
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.QuotSitePresheafModel` of the Quot presheaf in the universe of the site,
one of which always exists (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.QuotSituation.exists_quotSitePresheafModel`,
with no size hypothesis: the sets of quotients are small), and any two of which are isomorphic
over `B`.

## The size condition in the first assertion

On the size-bounded site the first assertion for an *arbitrary* quasi-coherent `F` is false.
Over the spectrum of a separably closed field of the site, with `X = B = S`, `f = 𝟙` and `F` free
of rank the cutoff of the site plus one, the Quot functor has at least `2 ^ cutoff` points over
`S`, while an algebraic space of the site has fewer than `cutoff`. The source avoids this because
its set-theoretic conventions let the big site grow with the data (`errata.md`, entry 09TU(1)).
The first assertion is therefore proved for `F` étale-locally generated by fewer than `μ` sections
for some `μ` below the cutoff of the site. Every `F` of finite type satisfies this condition
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_isEtaleLocallyGeneratedBelow_lt_cutoff_of_isFiniteTypeOnSite`),
and so does every `F` the source applies 09TU to. The second assertion carries no such
condition, since an `F` of finite presentation is of finite type.

## The proof

This is the source's proof. `Coh_{X/B}` is an algebraic stack (Stacks 09DS). The 1-morphism
`Quot_{F/X/B} ⟶ Coh_{X/B}` sending `F_T ⟶ Q` to `Q` is representable by algebraic spaces: its
fibre over `(T, 𝒢)` is the open subspace (Stacks 09TP) of surjections inside the relative Hom
space `Hom(F_T, 𝒢)` (Stacks 08K6). Hence the stack in setoids `Quot_{F/X/B}` is algebraic
(Stacks 05UM), and its sheaf is an algebraic space (Stacks 04SZ). Grothendieck's existence theorem
for algebraic spaces (Stacks 0CXB), which the algebraicity of `Coh_{X/B}` consumes, is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.InverseSystemExistenceSituation.exists_flat_algebraization`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.QuotSituation.isAlgebraicSpace_and_isLocallyOfFinitePresentation_quot`:
  **Stacks 09TU**, both assertions.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.QuotSituation.isAlgebraicSpace_quot_of_isFiniteTypeOnSite`: the first
  assertion for `F` of finite type.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09TU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09TU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09DS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09DS)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08K6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08K6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0CXB](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0CXB)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
